Module 7: Intro to Structured Queries

Overview: This module builds on relational database fundamentals and focuses on how to structure, relate, and manipulate data across multiple tables using SQL.
How database schemas are designed, how keys define relationships, and how to combine and modify data efficiently using structured queries.


Key Concepts
Database Schema
1. A database schema defines: Tables, Columns, Data types, Keys (Primary and Foreign), Relationships between tables
2. Schemas help organize data efficiently and reduce redundancy.

Primary Keys and Foreign Keys
Primary Key (PK): Uniquely identifies each row in a table. Cannot contain duplicate or NULL values

Foreign Key (FK): Links one table to another. References a primary key in another table
These keys allow relational databases to connect related data.

Database Relationships
1. One-to-Many: One record in Table A relates to many records in Table B.
2. Many-to-Many: Many records in Table A relate to many records in Table B.
3. One-to-One: One record in Table A relates to one record in Table B.


SQL JOIN Operations
JOIN allows combining data from multiple tables.

Example:

SELECT patients.name, admissions.date
FROM patients
INNER JOIN admissions
ON patients.patient_id = admissions.patient_id;

Types of JOINs
INNER JOIN: Returns only matching rows in both tables.

LEFT JOIN: Returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right table.

RIGHT JOIN: Returns all rows from the right table and matching rows from the left table.

FULL OUTER JOIN: Returns all rows from both tables, with NULL where no match exists.

NATURAL JOIN: Automatically joins tables using columns with the same name.

SQL Data Manipulation Language (DML): Used to modify data in tables.

Database Data Types
